              {
                "text": "I'm analyzing the training example's nested ring structure, where each shape (4, 0, 1, 7, 9, 8) forms a concentric layer from outer frame to center in the 11x11 output, and figuring out how each shape's corner fragment determines its ring's pattern with 4-fold symmetry.\n\nI'm mapping shape 4's fragment to the outermost ring (size 11), shape 0's fragment to the next ring (offset by 1), and shape 1's fragment (a diagonal 2-cell L-shape) to the following ring, confirming each fragment's corner shape gets replicated symmetrically at successive insets.\n\nContinuing this pattern, shape 7's fragment fits at inset 3 forming the next ring, and shape 9's corner quadrant fills the 3x3 ring at inset 4, leaving just the center cell for shape 8.\n\nNow I'm trying to figure out what determines the ordering of shapes rather than the specific placement. Since most fragments are the same 2x2 size, the size of shape 4's fragment being uniquely larger doesn't explain the sequence, so I suspect the ordering is actually encoded in how the shapes are arranged as corner fragments within the input grid itself, which seems to have its own 4-fold symmetric structure worth examining.\n\n7's copies span a 5x5 region, matching a ring size, while 4's span 11x11 \u2014 so maybe ring size corresponds to the span of each digit's scattered copies. But 0's copies don't form a clean span, so I'm unsure this pattern holds consistently. Perhaps instead I should just nest shapes by size using the explicit examples: 9 as 3x3, 8 as 1x1, leaving 0 and 1 sizes uncertain.\n\nI don't have enough basis for the ordering rule yet, so I turn to the test grid itself, which uses colors 8, 5, 4, 9, 2, 3 with background 1. I start mapping out the rows, tracking where each colored shape (4, 8, 5, 9) appears across the grid to figure out their positions and extents.\n\nContinuing to map rows, I find colors 2 and 3 appearing in additional patterns, and I piece together that color 8 forms a 9x9 ring shape occupying rows 4-12 and columns 0-8, with each corner being a 3x3 L-shaped quadrant. Color 5 appears as small 2x2-ish blocks near the top corners of that ring.\n\nMeanwhile shape 4 also forms a similar 2x2 quadrant pattern mirrored vertically, shape 9 is just a single center cell, shape 2 forms a 3x3 ring nested inside, and shape 3 spans a 5x5 area on the right side with a ring-like arrangement across rows 8-12 and columns 12-15.\n\nLet me reconsider \u2014 maybe the stray 3s are leftover fragments from another overlapping shape, and the actual ring for the \"3\" shape is a 5x5 with a simple L-shaped quadrant. So the sizes seem to follow a pattern: 8 giving a 9x9 ring, 3 giving something smaller.\n\nThen checking the 4 and 5 shapes, I trace their duplicated quadrant positions across rows and columns to find their spans: 4's copies span 7 rows, suggesting a 7x7 ring, and 5's copies span 11 columns, suggesting an 11x11 ring \u2014 matching the training pattern where bigger numbers correspond to bigger ring sizes.\n\nNow assembling the 11x11 grid with nested square rings of sizes 11,9,7,5,3,1 for values 5,8,4,3,2,9, each inset by one from the previous, placing each ring's quadrant pattern (L-shapes and partial squares) into the four corners at the appropriate offset.\n\nNow I'm mirroring the grid horizontally column by column, reflecting each row's values across the center to build out the full symmetric pattern.\n\nThe 9x9 ring spans rows1-9 and cols1-9, and checking against the training pattern, the outer 4x4 quadrant fill in row0 matches what I'd expect, so the structure seems consistent.",
